CrowSetup -- installer/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CrowSetup.exe (and CrowSetup on Linux) is a Rust program. Its release binary
compiles in crates from crates.io -- MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, Zlib,
Unicode-3.0, CDLA-Permissive-2.0 (webpki-roots) and bzip2-1.0.6 (libbz2-rs-sys),
several dual-licensed -- and, on Windows, Microsoft's WebView2 loader:
webview2-com-sys links WebView2LoaderStatic.lib from the NuGet package
Microsoft.Web.WebView2 1.0.3800.47, under Microsoft's BSD-style licence.

One crate of the Linux build is under the Mozilla Public License 2.0, a
file-level copyleft: option-ext (through wry, dirs and dirs-sys), compiled in
unmodified. MPL-2.0 section 3.2(a) asks that recipients of the binary learn
where its source is; the notices file names the published .crate on crates.io
with the sha256 Cargo.lock pins, for every MPL crate the lock contains.

Every one of those texts, with the crate and version it belongs to, is in
`installer/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt`. The exe ships alone as a release asset, so
it carries that file too: `CrowSetup --licenses` prints it. The file is generated
from installer/Cargo.lock by `tools/installer_notices.py`, which also holds it
against the lock; texts the crate sources do not carry (webview2-com, dlopen2,
Microsoft's) are copied byte for byte into `installer/licenses/`, each with its
source and sha256 in `installer/licenses/sources.json`.

The Windows build also embeds two files it unpacks unchanged: python.org's
Python 3.13 embeddable package (PSF License Agreement; its LICENSE.txt is inside
the package and lands beside python.exe) and get-pip.py from pypa/get-pip
(MIT; the pip wheel inside it carries pip's licence and those of the libraries
pip vendors).
